1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,581
This is ECE 358, video 20b. It continues video 20a. The topic is BGP route selection and policy.

2
00:00:11,031 --> 00:00:16,706
After this video, you can name the BGP messages and the route selection order.

3
00:00:17,156 --> 00:00:19,716
You can explain hot potato routing.

4
00:00:20,166 --> 00:00:25,499
You can explain policy-based routing with import and export advertisements.

5
00:00:25,949 --> 00:00:30,088
Then we answer question bank questions 86 to 100.

6
00:00:30,538 --> 00:00:40,415
BGP peers, here 2c and 3a, exchange BGP messages over a TCP connection. There are four message types.

7
00:00:40,865 --> 00:00:46,732
The first is OPEN. It opens the TCP connection to the remote BGP peer.

8
00:00:47,182 --> 00:00:50,190
OPEN also authenticates the sending peer.

9
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:55,397
The second is KEEPALIVE. One use is to acknowledge the OPEN request.

10
00:00:55,847 --> 00:00:59,282
The third is UPDATE. It advertises a new path.

11
00:00:59,732 --> 00:01:02,335
UPDATE can also withdraw an old path.

12
00:01:02,785 --> 00:01:09,889
KEEPALIVE has a second use. When there are no UPDATE messages, KEEPALIVE messages keep the connection alive.

13
00:01:10,339 --> 00:01:15,160
The fourth is NOTIFICATION. It reports errors in a previous message.

14
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:19,279
NOTIFICATION is also used to close the connection.

15
00:01:19,729 --> 00:01:27,943
A BGP router may learn more than one route to the same destination. Here it learns routes 1, 2 and 3 to X.

16
00:01:28,393 --> 00:01:36,585
The router must select one route. It uses rules in a fixed order. It goes to the next rule only if there is still a tie.

17
00:01:37,035 --> 00:01:44,075
Rule 1: the local preference value attribute. This is a policy decision of the AS admin.

18
00:01:44,525 --> 00:01:50,903
Rule 2: the shortest AS-PATH, that is, the path through the fewest ASes.

19
00:01:51,353 --> 00:01:58,777
Rule 3: the closest NEXT-HOP router. This is called hot potato routing. The next slide explains it.

20
00:01:59,227 --> 00:02:05,201
Rule 4: additional criteria. Example: prefer the oldest path, for stability.

21
00:02:05,651 --> 00:02:08,509
At the end, the router has one selected route.

22
00:02:08,959 --> 00:02:19,519
Here AS2 gets two routes to the same destination network X. Router 2a gets the path AS1, AS3, X from AS1.

23
00:02:19,969 --> 00:02:24,449
And router 2c gets the path AS3, X from AS3.

24
00:02:24,899 --> 00:02:34,649
The orange numbers are the OSPF link weights inside AS2: 112, 201 and 263.

25
00:02:35,099 --> 00:02:43,312
Router 2d learns by iBGP that it can route to X through gateway 2a, or through gateway 2c.

26
00:02:43,762 --> 00:02:47,965
The intra-domain cost from 2d to 2a is 201.

27
00:02:48,415 --> 00:02:53,279
The intra-domain cost from 2d to 2c is 263.

28
00:02:53,729 --> 00:02:59,297
Hot potato routing chooses the local gateway with the least intra-domain cost.

29
00:02:59,747 --> 00:03:05,933
201 is less than 263. So 2d chooses gateway 2a.

30
00:03:06,383 --> 00:03:16,666
2d does this even though the path through 2a has more AS hops to X: two ASes, AS1 and AS3, not one.

31
00:03:17,116 --> 00:03:26,097
Hot potato routing does not consider the inter-domain cost. It gets the packet out of its own AS as fast as possible.

32
00:03:26,547 --> 00:03:36,211
This slide applies hot potato alone. In the full BGP selection order, local preference and the shortest AS-PATH come first.

33
00:03:36,661 --> 00:03:44,640
BGP uses advertisements to apply policy. In this picture, A, B and C are provider networks.

34
00:03:45,090 --> 00:03:48,162
w, x and y are customer networks.

35
00:03:48,612 --> 00:04:01,028
Here is a typical policy. An ISP only wants to route traffic to and from its own customer networks. It does not want to carry transit traffic between other ISPs.

36
00:04:01,478 --> 00:04:05,873
Provider A advertises the path A, w to B and to C.

37
00:04:06,323 --> 00:04:10,739
B chooses not to advertise the path B, A, w to C.

38
00:04:11,189 --> 00:04:19,359
B would get no revenue for routing C, B, A, w, because none of C, A and w are customers of B.

39
00:04:19,809 --> 00:04:23,820
So C does not learn about the path C, B, A, w.

40
00:04:24,270 --> 00:04:29,113
C routes C, A, w to reach w. It does not use B.

41
00:04:29,563 --> 00:04:37,456
Here is a second policy example. Customer network x is dual-homed. It is attached to two networks, B and C.

42
00:04:37,906 --> 00:04:43,538
The policy to enforce: x does not want to route traffic from B to C through x.

43
00:04:43,988 --> 00:04:47,103
So x will not advertise to B a route to C.

44
00:04:47,553 --> 00:04:50,390
Then B never learns a path B, x, C.

45
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:54,659
x still advertises its own prefix to B and to C.

46
00:04:55,109 --> 00:05:00,847
So both providers can reach x, but no traffic between B and C goes through x.

47
00:05:01,297 --> 00:05:09,852
The Internet uses one kind of routing inside an AS, and another kind between ASes. There are three reasons.

48
00:05:10,302 --> 00:05:21,054
The first reason is policy. Between ASes, an admin wants control over how its traffic is routed, and over who routes through its network.

49
00:05:21,504 --> 00:05:26,731
Inside an AS, there is a single admin. So policy is a non-issue.

50
00:05:27,181 --> 00:05:33,645
The second reason is scale. Hierarchical routing gives smaller tables and less update traffic.

51
00:05:34,095 --> 00:05:40,239
The third reason is performance. Inside an AS, routing can focus on performance.

52
00:05:40,689 --> 00:05:45,233
Between ASes, policy dominates over performance.

53
00:05:45,683 --> 00:05:54,259
Here is the Internet approach to scalable routing. The Internet is partitioned into an interconnected set of autonomous systems.

54
00:05:54,709 --> 00:06:05,397
For intra-AS routing, the AS admin chooses one intra-AS routing protocol. OSPF allows two-level hierarchical routing.

55
00:06:05,847 --> 00:06:14,977
Some routers, for example Cisco routers, also let an admin run more than one intra-AS protocol in the same AS.

56
00:06:15,427 --> 00:06:23,385
For inter-AS routing, all BGP routers run BGP, both eBGP and iBGP.

57
00:06:23,835 --> 00:06:29,104
The AS admin controls the packet flow with import and export policies.

58
00:06:29,554 --> 00:06:36,487
Also, routers continually aggregate routing table entries, as we saw with address aggregation.

59
00:06:36,937 --> 00:06:43,657
Now the question bank. The question bank has no published answers. These answers come from the slides.

60
00:06:44,107 --> 00:06:57,846
Question 86: what is the main idea in hierarchical OSPF? Question 87: what are the four types of routers in hierarchical OSPF? Pause the video and try it.

61
00:06:58,296 --> 00:07:06,552
Answer to 86. Hierarchical OSPF uses a two-level hierarchy: local areas and a backbone.

62
00:07:07,002 --> 00:07:13,061
Link-state advertisements are flooded only inside one area, or inside the backbone.

63
00:07:13,511 --> 00:07:17,116
This reduces the flooding overhead in a large AS.

64
00:07:17,566 --> 00:07:24,051
Answer to 87. The first type is local routers, also called internal routers.

65
00:07:24,501 --> 00:07:27,040
The second type is area border routers.

66
00:07:27,490 --> 00:07:29,730
The third type is backbone routers.

67
00:07:30,180 --> 00:07:35,257
The fourth type is boundary routers. They connect to other ASes.

68
00:07:35,707 --> 00:07:50,534
Questions 88 to 91. What is the main function of BGP? What are the two parts of BGP? What are the two parts of a BGP route? What is an AS-PATH? Pause the video and try it.

69
00:07:50,984 --> 00:08:01,907
Answer to 88. BGP is the inter-AS routing protocol. With BGP, an AS advertises the prefixes that it can reach.

70
00:08:02,357 --> 00:08:09,674
Each router learns paths to other networks. It selects good paths from reachability information and policy.

71
00:08:10,124 --> 00:08:18,252
Answer to 89. eBGP gets subnet reachability information from neighbouring ASes.

72
00:08:18,702 --> 00:08:24,889
iBGP propagates this information to the routers inside the same AS.

73
00:08:25,339 --> 00:08:33,317
Answer to 90. A BGP route is a prefix plus attributes. The prefix is the destination network.

74
00:08:33,767 --> 00:08:37,650
The two important attributes are AS-PATH and NEXT-HOP.

75
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:49,108
Answer to 91. The AS-PATH is the list of ASes that the prefix advertisement has passed through. Example: AS2, AS3.

76
00:08:49,558 --> 00:09:02,699
Question 92. A BGP router learns many paths to the same destination prefix. Which factors does it use to select one? List them in order. Pause the video and try it.

77
00:09:03,149 --> 00:09:07,885
Answer. First, the local preference value, which is a policy decision.

78
00:09:08,335 --> 00:09:10,426
Second, the shortest AS-PATH.

79
00:09:10,876 --> 00:09:15,399
Third, the closest NEXT-HOP router, which is hot potato routing.

80
00:09:15,849 --> 00:09:19,987
Fourth, additional criteria, for example the oldest path.

81
00:09:20,437 --> 00:09:24,213
The router uses the next factor only when there is a tie.

82
00:09:24,663 --> 00:09:40,279
Questions 93 to 95. Explain policy-based routing by BGP. How is it different from routing on reachability information? What are the two kinds of BGP policies? Pause the video and try it.

83
00:09:40,729 --> 00:09:47,855
Answer to 93 and 95. The admin of an AS controls routes with two kinds of policy.

84
00:09:48,305 --> 00:09:55,430
An import policy accepts or declines a received path. Example: never route through AS Y.

85
00:09:55,880 --> 00:10:01,128
An export policy decides whether to advertise a learned path to a neighbour.

86
00:10:01,578 --> 00:10:07,103
Example: an ISP does not carry transit traffic between other ISPs.

87
00:10:07,553 --> 00:10:14,081
Answer to 94. Reachability information only tells that a path to a prefix exists.

88
00:10:14,531 --> 00:10:21,550
Policy can reject a reachable path, even the shortest one, for administrative or business reasons.

89
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:28,187
Question 96. Briefly explain hot potato routing. Pause the video and try it.

90
00:10:28,637 --> 00:10:43,933
Answer. A router learns by iBGP that it can reach a destination through more than one gateway of its own AS. In the slide example, router 2d can use gateway 2a or gateway 2c.

91
00:10:44,383 --> 00:10:49,460
Hot potato routing chooses the gateway with the least intra-domain cost.

92
00:10:49,910 --> 00:10:56,779
So 2d chooses 2a, with cost 201, not 2c, with cost 263.

93
00:10:57,229 --> 00:11:08,259
The router does not consider the inter-domain cost, for example the number of AS hops. It sends the packet out of its own AS as fast as possible.

94
00:11:08,709 --> 00:11:20,890
Question 97. What are the benefits of using BGP between ASes and hierarchical OSPF inside an AS? Pause the video and try it.

95
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:31,793
Answer, from slide 136. First, policy. BGP lets each AS control how its traffic is routed, and who routes through it.

96
00:11:32,243 --> 00:11:38,174
Inside one AS there is a single admin, so OSPF does not need policy.

97
00:11:38,624 --> 00:11:43,723
Second, scale. The hierarchy gives smaller tables and less update traffic.

98
00:11:44,173 --> 00:11:47,309
OSPF areas also limit the flooding.

99
00:11:47,759 --> 00:11:55,375
Third, performance. Inside the AS, OSPF can focus on performance and use least-cost paths.

100
00:11:55,825 --> 00:11:59,089
Between ASes, policy comes first.

101
00:11:59,539 --> 00:12:10,013
Question 98. Here is the topology from the question bank. A, B, C, A2, B2 and C2 are provider ASes.

102
00:12:10,463 --> 00:12:16,629
w, x, y, w2, x2 and y2 are customer ASes.

103
00:12:17,079 --> 00:12:25,676
Design policies for B2, so that B2 does not carry packets between w and x2. Pause the video and try it.

104
00:12:26,126 --> 00:12:31,779
Step 1. Find the paths from w to x2 that use B2. There are two.

105
00:12:32,229 --> 00:12:35,685
The first path is w, A, C, B2, x2.

106
00:12:36,135 --> 00:12:39,911
The second path is w, A, A2, B2, x2.

107
00:12:40,361 --> 00:12:45,951
These paths exist only if B2 advertises a path to x2 to its neighbours.

108
00:12:46,401 --> 00:12:51,542
Step 2. x2 is dual-homed. It is attached to B2 and to C2.

109
00:12:51,992 --> 00:12:58,541
So w can still reach x2 without B2: w, A, A2, C2, x2.

110
00:12:58,991 --> 00:13:08,378
Step 3. Export policy 1. B2 does not advertise any path to x2 to its provider neighbours C, A2 and C2.

111
00:13:08,828 --> 00:13:20,775
BGP advertises per destination prefix. So no AS on the side of w learns a path to x2 through B2. The two red paths are gone.

112
00:13:21,225 --> 00:13:31,401
Step 4. The other direction, from x2 to w. Export policy 2: B2 does not advertise to x2 any path to w.

113
00:13:31,851 --> 00:13:36,437
Or, with an import policy, B2 rejects the paths to w.

114
00:13:36,887 --> 00:13:46,679
Then x2 sends its packets for w through C2. So traffic between w and x2 goes through C2 in both directions.

115
00:13:47,129 --> 00:13:57,455
This uses the method of slides 138 and 139. It is one possible answer. The question bank has no published answer.

116
00:13:57,905 --> 00:14:07,014
Question 99. Identify and explain four network layer strategies that make the Internet scalable. Pause the video and try it.

117
00:14:07,464 --> 00:14:14,973
Answer, from slide 140. Strategy 1: the Internet is partitioned into autonomous systems.

118
00:14:15,423 --> 00:14:23,274
Routing has two levels, intra-AS and inter-AS. So no router needs the full topology of the Internet.

119
00:14:23,724 --> 00:14:30,423
Strategy 2: hierarchical OSPF. An AS is split into areas and a backbone.

120
00:14:30,873 --> 00:14:33,710
Link-state flooding stays inside one area.

121
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:46,405
Strategy 3: BGP between ASes. BGP routers exchange reachability, as prefixes with AS-PATHs, not the full topology.

122
00:14:46,855 --> 00:14:50,439
Import and export policies limit which paths spread.

123
00:14:50,889 --> 00:15:00,297
Strategy 4: route aggregation. With hierarchical CIDR addresses, a router can advertise one prefix for many subnets.

124
00:15:00,747 --> 00:15:02,902
This makes forwarding tables smaller.

125
00:15:03,352 --> 00:15:08,387
NAT is one more strategy from earlier: it saves IPv4 address space.

126
00:15:08,837 --> 00:15:15,941
Question 100. Compare OSPF and BGP in a table. Pause the video and try it.

127
00:15:16,391 --> 00:15:20,039
Row 1, scope. OSPF is intra-AS.

128
00:15:20,489 --> 00:15:22,643
BGP is inter-AS.

129
00:15:23,093 --> 00:15:36,320
Be careful: the example row in the question bank shows these two swapped. The slides say OSPF is intra-AS routing, and BGP is routing among ASes.

130
00:15:36,770 --> 00:15:42,850
Row 2, the algorithm. OSPF is link-state routing with Dijkstra's algorithm.

131
00:15:43,300 --> 00:15:46,351
BGP is a path vector protocol.

132
00:15:46,801 --> 00:15:52,433
Row 3, the information sent. OSPF floods link-state advertisements.

133
00:15:52,883 --> 00:15:58,643
BGP sends prefixes with attributes, such as AS-PATH and NEXT-HOP.

134
00:15:59,093 --> 00:16:04,511
Row 4, transport. OSPF messages go directly over IP.

135
00:16:04,961 --> 00:16:09,847
BGP messages go over TCP connections between peers.

136
00:16:10,297 --> 00:16:17,614
Row 5, route choice. OSPF picks least-cost paths, with costs such as bandwidth or delay.

137
00:16:18,064 --> 00:16:24,315
BGP uses policy first, then the shortest AS-PATH, then hot potato.

138
00:16:24,765 --> 00:16:30,311
Row 6, structure. OSPF can use two levels: areas and a backbone.

139
00:16:30,761 --> 00:16:35,519
BGP has two parts: eBGP and iBGP.

140
00:16:35,969 --> 00:16:43,414
Recap. The Internet groups routers into autonomous systems, for scale and for administrative autonomy.

141
00:16:43,864 --> 00:16:48,621
Inside an AS, OSPF runs, with areas and a backbone.

142
00:16:49,071 --> 00:16:55,237
Between ASes, BGP runs, with eBGP and iBGP.

143
00:16:55,687 --> 00:17:03,623
A BGP router selects a route by local preference, then the shortest AS-PATH, then hot potato.

144
00:17:04,073 --> 00:17:07,315
Import and export policies control the routes.

145
00:17:07,765 --> 00:17:16,576
Each forwarding table combines both levels. BGP gives the gateway, and OSPF gives the interface toward it.

146
00:17:17,026 --> 00:17:24,962
Advertisements go in one direction, and data goes in the other direction. This is the end of video 20b.
